{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-5493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=5$, the second term is ${a}_{2}=15$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=845.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-5493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know the first two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =15-5 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=5$, $d=10$, and ${S}_{N}=845$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 845 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5)+(N-1)(10)) \\\\ 845 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+10N-10) \\\\ 845 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10N) \\\\ 845 & =5{N}^{2} \\\\ 169 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-5493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0b507e595c972b45c685bacfe2a7421de4aba779468e93b336c0581a0f1e9f7e"} {"question" "q-5493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-5493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "19"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-5493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n1+3+5+…=81\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=81$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=1.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2+2N-2) \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N) \\\\ 81 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=9$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0b507e595c972b45c685bacfe2a7421de4aba779468e93b336c0581a0f1e9f7e"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+15+25+…=180\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=180$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =15-5 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot 10) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+10N-10) \\\\ 180 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10N) \\\\ 180 & =5{N}^{2} \\\\ 36 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=6$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0b507e595c972b45c685bacfe2a7421de4aba779468e93b336c0581a0f1e9f7e"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-67074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n4.2+4.7+5.2+...=139.4\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=139.4,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=4.2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4.7-4.2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 139.4 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4.2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(8.4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(7.9+0.5N) \\\\ 278.8 & =7.9N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+7.9N-278.8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm \\sqrt{(-7.9)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-278.8)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm 24.9}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=17$ or $N=-32.8.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive and integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=17$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0b507e595c972b45c685bacfe2a7421de4aba779468e93b336c0581a0f1e9f7e"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "33"] ["math" "35"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "17"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An arithmetic series has the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=17,$ the $9$th term ${a}_{9}=26,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=77.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{6}}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{26-17}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that ${a}_{6}=17.$ We can compute ${a}_{1}$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+(6-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+15 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=2,$ $d=3,$ and ${S}_{N}=77$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 77 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 3) \\\\ 154 & =N(4+3N-3) \\\\ 154 & =N(3N+1) \\\\ 154 & =3{N}^{2}+N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+N-154 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{(1)^{2}-4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-154)}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{1\\,849}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1\\pm 43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=7$ or $N=-\\frac{22}{3}.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=7$ terms." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/state.json" "file_sha256" "adbf1e3d0bc2a2f1dc718d724b566a99ea37d87056c7748a86fdb41c34b927f6"} {"question" "q-108939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061988/activity-metadata.json" 